course: dictionary
lesson: 10954
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word walkabout is a good example. Read the words around it first, because they show you which meaning fits.
explain: «In a walkabout visitors walk among kangaroos and wallabies on paths with no fence.» Here the sentence is about animals and paths, so walkabout means a walk among the animals, not a royal visit. Try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: A walkabout can also be a royal walk, when a king, queen or famous person walks among a crowd, shaking hands and talking. Informally, if a pet or a child goes walkabout, they have wandered off and gone missing. Each meaning fits a different kind of sentence.
explain: Walkabout also has a traditional meaning in Australia. For some Aboriginal Australians, it is a journey on foot through the bush, linked to culture, country and learning. Speak about it with respect, because it is an important part of their culture.
fact: Walkabout is a noun, so you can say a walkabout or walkabouts.
fact: In Australia, a walkabout is a traditional journey on foot through the bush, linked to culture, country and learning.
fact: In the UK, a walkabout is when a king, queen or famous person walks among a crowd, shaking hands and talking.
fact: Informally, to «go walkabout» means to wander off or go missing.
fact: A «royal walkabout» is a walk among the crowd during a royal visit.
exercise: The royal visitor did a walkabout in the square, shaking hands with the crowd. What is a walkabout here? | A long journey through the bush | *A walk among a crowd, shaking hands and talking | A ticket for a train | A kind of small car
exercise: Mum says, «Where is the dog? He's gone walkabout again!» What does gone walkabout mean? | Gone to the shops with the family | *Wandered off and gone missing | Gone for a swim in the lake | Gone to sleep in the garden
exercise: «In a walkabout visitors walk among kangaroos and wallabies on paths with no fence.» What do the visitors do? | Ride on the backs of the animals | *Walk among the animals on the paths | Feed the animals from a boat | Build a fence around the paths
test: Read this: «The queen's royal walkabout drew cheering crowds.» Which meaning fits? | *A walk among the crowd, shaking hands and talking | A path with a fence around it | Wandering off and going missing | A journey on foot through the bush
test: Jayden says walkabout always means a royal walk. Is he right? | No, it only means a walk in the park | Yes, but only in Australia | *No, it has several meanings, and the context decides which one | Yes, it always means a royal walk
exam: Which meaning of walkabout is the traditional Australian one, to be spoken of with respect? | A path through a shopping centre | A royal walk among a crowd | *A journey on foot through the bush, linked to culture, country and learning | Wandering off and going missing
